<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Section 2.4 - Linear independence</title>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A set of vectors is called <em>linearly dependent</em> if one of the
        vectors is a linear combination of the others.
        Otherwise, the set of vectors is called <em>linearly independent</em>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the colors activity we did on the first day.
        Let's use the elementary school art definitions, say red, blue and
        yellow are primary colors (and together can make any color), and purple,
        green, and orange are secondary colors (that can be made of two primary
        colors).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What would you say it means for a set of colors to span all colors?
          What would you say it means for a set of colors to be linearly
          independent?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Span all colors - we can make any color with the set.
          Linearly independent - no color in the set can be made with other
          colors in the set.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put any number of colors (of red, blue, yellow, purple, green, and
          orange) in each box below satisfying the appropriate conditions, or
          put X if it’s impossible.
          Explain your reasoning.
        </p>

        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="70%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>Linearly Dependent</cell>
            <cell>Linearly Independent</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is all colors</cell>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is not all colors</cell>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          For (1), B + Y = G, so they are linearly dependent.
          But, can make all colors, so it spans.
          For (2), we can make all colors with R, B, and Y, so it spans.
          Also, these are all primary colors and can't be used to make each
          other, so are linearly independent.
          For (3), R + B = P, so linearly dependent.
          It does not span since we can't make yellow.
          For (4), can't make yellow, so this set doesn't span and we can't make
          red or blue with the others, so linearly independent.
        </p>

        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="70%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>Linearly Dependent</cell>
            <cell>Linearly Independent</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is all colors</cell>
            <cell>(1) R, B, Y, G</cell>
            <cell>(2) R, B, Y</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is not all colors</cell>
            <cell>(3) R, B, P</cell>
            <cell>(4) R, B</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Thinking in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> now - can a set of two vectors be
          linearly dependent?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes, <m>\{\twovec{0}{1}, \twovec{0}{2}\}</m> is linearly dependent,
          since <m>2\twovec{0}{1} = \twovec{0}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        We would like to develop a means to detect when a set of vectors is
        linearly depen- dent.
        This activity will point the way.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that we have five vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> that form the
          columns of a matrix having reduced row echelon form
          <md>
            \[\vvec_1 \,\,\, \vvec_2 \,\,\, \vvec_3 \,\,\, \vvec_4 \,\,\, \vvec_5 \] \sim 
         \begin{bmatrix}
         1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
         0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
         0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
         0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
         \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Is it possible to write one of the vectors
          <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2,
         \vvec_3, \vvec_4, \vvec_5</m> as a
          linear combination of the others?
          If so, show explicitly how one vector appears as a linear combination
          of some of the other vectors.
          Is this set of vectors linearly dependent or independent?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes, it is possible!
          <m>\vvec_3 = -\vvec_1 + 2\vvec_2</m> and thus, the set of these 3
          vectors is linearly dependent.
          Additionally, <m>\vvec_5</m> can be written as a linear combination of
          the other 4 vectors in infinitely many ways.
          Therefore, the set of all 5 vectors is linearly dependent.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Put any number of colors (of red, blue, yellow, purple, green, and
          orange) in each box below satisfying the appropriate conditions, or
          put X if it’s impossible.
          Explain your reasoning.
        </p>

        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="70%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>Linearly Dependent</cell>
            <cell>Linearly Independent</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is all colors</cell>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is not all colors</cell>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          For (1), B + Y = G, so they are linearly dependent.
          But, can make all colors, so it spans.
          For (2), we can make all colors with R, B, and Y, so it spans.
          Also, these are all primary colors and can't be used to make each
          other, so are linearly independent.
          For (3), R + B = P, so linearly dependent.
          It does not span since we can't make yellow.
          For (4), can't make yellow, so this set doesn't span and we can't make
          red or blue with the others, so linearly independent.
        </p>

        <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="70%"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell/>
            <cell>Linearly Dependent</cell>
            <cell>Linearly Independent</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is all colors</cell>
            <cell>(1) R, B, Y, G</cell>
            <cell>(2) R, B, Y</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Span is not all colors</cell>
            <cell>(3) R, B, P</cell>
            <cell>(4) R, B</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Thinking in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> now - can a set of two vectors be
          linearly dependent?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes, <m>\{\twovec{0}{1}, \twovec{0}{2}\}</m> is linearly dependent,
          since <m>2\twovec{0}{1} = \twovec{0}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the matrix
        <md>
          A =
  \left[
    \begin{array}{ccc}
      3 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
      -1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\
      2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
        </md>
        whose columns are the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and
        <m>\vvec_3</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          [label=(\alph*)]
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a parametric description of the solution space to the equation
          <m>A\xvec = \zerovec</m>.
          (This equation is called ``homogeneous" because of the
          <m>\zerovec</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find one set of nonzero weights <m>c_1</m>, <m>c_2</m>, <m>c_3</m>
          such that
          <md>
            c_1\vvec_1 + c_2\vvec_2 + c_3\vvec_3 = \zerovec.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use these weights to show that one of the vectors can be written as a
          linear combination of the others.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and
          <m>\vvec_3</m> are linearly dependent.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Do the vectors <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and <m>\vvec_3</m> span
          <m>\real^3</m>?
          (Hint: Use part (a).)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is any matrix, explain why the homogeneous equation
          <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m> is consistent.
          For instance, is there a vector you can guarantee is a solution?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What condition on the pivots of <m>A</m> guarantees that there is
          solution to the homogeneous equation <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m> besides
          the solution <m>\xvec=\zerovec</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the example above, we saw that having a nonzero solution to the
          homogeneous equation <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m> enabled us to conclude
          that the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly dependent.
          What condition on the pivots of <m>A</m> will guarantee that the
          columns of <m>A</m> are linearly dependent?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the matrix
        <m>A=
  \left[
    \begin{array}{ccc}
      0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right]
  </m>
        whose columns are <m>\vvec_1</m>, <m>\vvec_2</m>, and <m>\vvec_3</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          [label=(\alph*)]
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the vectors below.
          Do you think the vectors are linearly independent or linearly
          dependent?
        </p>

        <p>
          \includegraphics{empty-4.eps}
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain how we know that there is a nonzero solution to the
          homogeneous equation <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a nonzero solution to the homogeneous solution
          <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use this solution to express one of the vectors as a linear
          combination of the others.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          [label=(\alph*)]
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What condition on the pivots of a matrix <m>A</m> will guarantee that
          its columns are linearly independent?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> are linearly
          independent vectors in <m>\real^9</m>.
          What can you say about the number of vectors in this set?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the smallest number of vectors that can span
          <m>\real^{13}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the largest number of vectors in <m>\real^{13}</m> that are
          linearly independent?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that a set of vectors in <m>\real^{13}</m> is linearly
          independent and spans <m>\real^{13}</m>.
          What can you say about the number of vectors?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <outcomes>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          What does it mean for a set of vectors to be linearly dependent?
          Linearly independent?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          What can you say about the pivots of a matrix if the columns of that
          matrix are a linearly dependent set of vectors?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          What is the maximum number of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m> that can
          be linearly dependent?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          When there is a non-trivial solution to the homogeneous equation
          <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m>, what can you say about the columns of the
          matrix <m>A</m> (in terms of linear dependence/independence)?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </outcomes>
</worksheet>